# Architecture Guidelines

## Data Access Layer

- Implement class-based data access layer
- Keep database logic isolated from UI components
- Use Drizzle ORM for all database operations
- Implement proper error handling and logging
- Use connection pooling with Neon Database

## Server/Client Boundaries

- Prefer React Server Components where possible
- Use "use client" directive only when necessary
- Keep client-side JavaScript minimal
- Use server actions for data mutations
- Implement proper error handling for server actions

## State Management

- Use React's built-in state management when possible
- Avoid unnecessary global state
- Colocate state with the components that use it
- Use React Context for theme and authentication state

## Event Handlers

- Always use `onKeyDown` instead of the deprecated `onKeyPress` for keyboard event handling
- Use `onKeyDown` for Enter key detection and form submissions
- Replace any existing `onKeyPress` handlers with `onKeyDown` equivalents

```tsx
// ❌ Avoid deprecated onKeyPress
onKeyPress={(e) => e.key === "Enter" && handleSubmit()}

// ✅ Use onKeyDown instead
onKeyDown={(e) => e.key === "Enter" && !e.shiftKey && handleSubmit()}
```

## Optimistic Updates

- Use React's `useOptimistic` hook instead of `router.refresh()` for immediate UI feedback
- Implement optimistic updates for all user interactions that modify data
- Always provide fallback states when optimistic updates fail
- Pass optimistic state down from parent components managing the data

```tsx
// ✅ Implement optimistic updates
const [optimisticMessages, addOptimisticMessage] = useOptimistic(
  messages,
  (state, newMessage) => [...state, newMessage],
);

// Add optimistic message immediately
addOptimisticMessage({ id: tempId, content: message, status: "sending" });
```

## Form Handling with useActionState

- Use Next.js 15's `useActionState` hook for form submissions with server actions
- Wrap form inputs in proper `<form>` elements with action attributes
- Handle form state, pending states, and errors through the hook
- Use FormData for form submissions instead of controlled inputs when possible

```tsx
// ✅ Use useActionState for form handling
import { useActionState } from "react";

const [state, formAction, isPending] = useActionState(sendMessageAction, {
  message: "",
  error: null,
});

// Wrap in form element
<form action={formAction}>
  <input name="message" required />
  <button type="submit" disabled={isPending}>
    {isPending ? "Sending..." : "Send"}
  </button>
</form>;
```

## Error Handling with shadcn Toast

- Replace all `console.error` statements with proper user-facing error messages
- Use shadcn's toast component for error notifications
- Import toast from `@/components/ui/use-toast` or your configured toast hook
- Provide meaningful error messages to users
- Always handle both network and application errors

```tsx
// ✅ Proper error handling with toast
import { useToast } from "@/components/ui/use-toast";
import { tryCatch } from "@walkup/walkup-utils";

const { toast } = useToast();

const result = await tryCatch(
  () => sendMessageAction(conversationId, message),
  (error) => {
    toast({
      title: "Error",
      description: "Something went wrong. Please try again.",
      variant: "destructive",
    });
    return { success: false, error: error.message };
  },
);

if (!result.success) {
  toast({
    title: "Error",
    description: result.error || "Failed to send message",
    variant: "destructive",
  });
}
```

## Loading States with useTransition

- Use React's `useTransition` for non-blocking state transitions
- Implement `isPending` states for better user feedback
- Use `startTransition` to wrap state updates that might cause UI lag
- Combine with optimistic updates for the smoothest user experience
- Show loading indicators on buttons and forms during transitions

```tsx
// ✅ Use useTransition for loading states
import { useTransition } from "react";

const [isPending, startTransition] = useTransition();

const handleSubmit = () => {
  startTransition(async () => {
    // Non-blocking state update
    await sendMessage();
  });
};

// Show loading state in UI
<Button disabled={isPending}>{isPending ? "Sending..." : "Send"}</Button>;
```

## Combined Implementation Pattern

When implementing all these patterns together:

```tsx
"use client";

import { useOptimistic, useTransition } from "react";
import { useActionState } from "react";
import { useToast } from "@/components/ui/use-toast";
import { tryCatch } from "@walkup/walkup-utils";

export function MessageInput({ conversationId, messages }) {
  const { toast } = useToast();
  const [isPending, startTransition] = useTransition();

  const [optimisticMessages, addOptimisticMessage] = useOptimistic(
    messages,
    (state, newMessage) => [...state, newMessage],
  );

  const [state, formAction] = useActionState(
    async (prevState, formData) => {
      const message = formData.get("message") as string;

      // Add optimistic update
      addOptimisticMessage({
        id: Date.now(),
        content: message,
        status: "sending",
      });

      const result = await tryCatch(
        () => sendMessageAction(conversationId, message),
        (error) => {
          toast({
            title: "Error",
            description: "Something went wrong",
            variant: "destructive",
          });
          return { success: false, error: error.message };
        },
      );

      if (!result.success) {
        toast({
          title: "Error",
          description: "Failed to send message",
          variant: "destructive",
        });
      }
      return { success: result.success };
    },
    { success: true },
  );

  return (
    <form action={formAction}>
      <input
        name="message"
        onKeyDown={(e) => {
          if (e.key === "Enter" && !e.shiftKey) {
            e.preventDefault();
            startTransition(() => {
              formAction(new FormData(e.currentTarget.form));
            });
          }
        }}
      />
      <button type="submit" disabled={isPending}>
        {isPending ? "Sending..." : "Send"}
      </button>
    </form>
  );
}
```

## Data Fetching in React Server Components

- Always prefer server-side data fetching over client-side fetching
- Fetch data directly in React Server Components (RSC) using DAL methods
- Never use server actions for data fetching - only for mutations
- Use DAL methods directly in RSC components for optimal performance
- Leverage Next.js automatic caching and revalidation for data
- Keep data fetching logic in the component that needs the data
- Use `unstable_noStore()` only when data must be fresh on every request

```tsx
// ✅ Server-side data fetching in RSC
import { ToolDAL } from "@/lib/dal/tool.dal";

export default async function ToolPage({ params }: { params: { id: string } }) {
  const tool = await ToolDAL.getById(params.id);

  if (!tool) {
    notFound();
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>{tool.name}</h1>
      <p>{tool.description}</p>
      {/* Render tool details */}
    </div>
  );
}
```

```tsx
